on\":{\"ref\":\"d37\"},\"ref\":\"d37\",\"snippet\":\"\",\"title\":\"https://enkoeducation.com/the-must-read-in-african-literature/\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d36\"},\"ref\":\"d36\",\"snippet\":\"\",\"title\":\"https://www.english.uga.edu/directory/people/chigozie-obioma\"}],\"next_step\":\"Open a received ref with read; use replay=true to restore full cached navigation.\",\"observation\":{\"first_delivered_source_round\":10,\"prior_deliveries\":1,\"query_key\":\"c210bc73b7e81e4da0c7d8bce6f46a9d3abcfd42222ad24db3de9d5935e42251\",\"replay_requested\":false,\"result_fingerprint\":\"8a6bad53844ad8c16f0ea05c03223950f969908c5aaf33135a1b70b3f89467c1\",\"semantic_progress\":\"not_measured\"},\"query\":\"The Fishermen Chigozie Obioma 2015 Booker Prize shortlist\",\"view\":\"reuse\"}]}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_56635095e220497cb31eb959"},{"content":"I have a strong lead. From e1, I've confirmed:\n- Chigozie Obioma was born in Akure, Nigeria\n- His novels \"The Fishermen\" (2015) and \"An Orchestra of Minorities\" (2019) were shortlisted for the Booker Prize and translated into 30 languages (over 25 ✓)\n\nNow I need to verify the puzzle's structure. The puzzle says the award was \"originally created in the 2000s.\" The Booker Prize was created in 1969, which doesn't fit. But the puzzle might be using loose phrasing, OR there's a different interpretation. Let me reconsider: \"An Orchestra of Minorities\" (2019) was a Booker contender. In 2019, the Booker was won by \"The Testaments\" (Atwood) and \"Girl, Woman, Other\" (Evaristo). \n\nBut wait — the puzzle says Book B \"had been released the preceding year\" and won the award in the year Book A was published. \"The Testaments\" was published in 2019 (same year), not preceding. Let me reconsider whether the puzzle refers to the regular Booker or International Booker, and check the 2019 Booker winner details and whether any winner was published the preceding year.\n\nLet me also reconsider: maybe Book A is \"The Fishermen\" (2015), and in 2015 the Booker was won by \"A Brief History of Seven Killings\" (Marlon James, published 2014 — the preceding year!). Let me verify this and Marlon James's birthplace.","role":"assistant","tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"queries\": [\"A Brief History of Seven Killings Marlon James publication date 2014 Booker Prize 2015\", \"Marlon James birthplace born city\", \"The Fishermen Chigozie Obioma 2015 Booker Prize shortlist\"]}","name":"search"},"id":"call_3b412940a83644fdba0b3584","index":0,"type":"function"}]},{"content":"{\"action_slot_charged\":true,\"executed\":true,\"ok\":true,\"results\":[{\"cached\":true,\"hits\":[{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d43\"},\"ref\":\"d43\",\"snippet\":\"\",\"title\":\"https://www.ontheprize.co.uk/blog/the-booker-prize-in-the-2010s\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d82\"},\"ref\":\"d82\",\"snippet\":\"\",\"title\":\"https://www.grinnell.edu/campus-life/arts-culture/writersgrinnell/distinguished-authors\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d14\"},\"ref\":\"d14\",\"snippet\":\"\",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Booker_Prize\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d83\"},\"ref\":\"d83\",\"snippet\":\"\",\"title\":\"https://www.panmacmillan.com/blogs/literary/our-all-time-favourite-man-booker-prize-winning-novels\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d84\"},\"ref\":\"d84\",\"snippet\":\"\",\"title\":\"https://publishingperspectives.com/2024/07/the-uks-booker-prize-for-fiction-2024-longlist/\"}],\"next_step\":\"Open a received ref with read; use replay=true to restore full cached navigation.\",\"observation\":{\"first_delivered_source_round\":10,\"prior_deliveries\":2,\"query_key\":\"3f0db3d028dc551504e5424bfa4b5b60873ef24f4d90e55087cfd7266c2bb18a\",\"replay_requested\":false,\"result_fingerprint\":\"69c305c6bc87065f1d463134e435ec55470c91eafece8c93458c0d7dfd9e0a13\",\"semantic_progress\":\"not_measured\"},\"query\":\"A Brief History of Seven Killings Marlon James publication date 2014 Booker Prize 2015\",\"view\":\"reuse\"},{\"cached\":true,\"hits\":[{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d85\"},\"ref\":\"d85\",\"snippet\":\"\",\"title\":\"https://www.ranker.com/list/rock-stars-with-the-most-kids/celebrity-lists\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d86\"},\"ref\":\"d86\",\"snippet\":\"\",\"title\":\"https://www.oldest.org/culture/living-actors/\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d87\"},\"ref\":\"d87\",\"snippet\":\"\",\"title\":\"https://www.nojc.org/sandbar\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d88\"},\"ref\":\"d88\",\"snippet\":\"\",\"title\":\"https://www.biography.com/actors/a46180093/barry-keoghan\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d89\"},\"ref\":\"d89\",\"snippet\":\"\",\"title\":\"https://www.biography.com/musicians/michael-jackson\"}],\"next_step\":\"Open a received ref with read; use replay=true to restore full cached navigation.\",\"observation\":{\"first_delivered_source_round\":10,\"prior_deliveries\":2,\"query_key\":\"b00ad6a509a10aa1beb993153db57804082175527b0a43fe0475d64c8338ac4b\",\"replay_requested\":false,\"result_fingerprint\":\"78d04a0bebd0cc3f7947a3b3fdedc9e02fef8e9dc40785581d8bf78e0ed20949\",\"semantic_progress\":\"not_measured\"},\"query\":\"Marlon James birthplace born city\",\"view\":\"reuse\"},{\"cached\":true,\"hits\":[{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[{\"end\":3000,\"ref\":\"e1\",\"start\":0}],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d21\"},\"ref\":\"d21\",\"snippet\":\"\",\"title\":\"https://www.oxbelly.com/fiction-writers-program/advisors\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d15\"},\"ref\":\"d15\",\"snippet\":\"\",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_winners_and_nominated_authors_of_the_Booker_Prize\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d90\"},\"ref\":\"d90\",\"snippet\":\"\",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/African_literature\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d37\"},\"ref\":\"d37\",\"snippet\":\"\",\"title\":\"https://enkoeducation.com/the-must-read-in-african-literature/\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d36\"},\"ref\":\"d36\",\"snippet\":\"\",\"title\":\"https://www.english.uga.edu/directory/people/chigozie-obioma\"}],\"next_step\":\"Open a received ref with read; use replay=true to restore full cached navigation.\",\"observation\":{\"first_delivered_source_round\":10,\"prior_deliveries\":2,\"query_key\":\"c210bc73b7e81e4da0c7d8bce6f46a9d3abcfd42222ad24db3de9d5935e42251\",\"replay_requested\":false,\"result_fingerprint\":\"8a6bad53844ad8c16f0ea05c03223950f969908c5aaf33135a1b70b3f89467c1\",\"semantic_progress\":\"not_measured\"},\"query\":\"The Fishermen Chigozie Obioma 2015 Booker Prize shortlist\",\"view\":\"reuse\"}]}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_3b412940a83644fdba0b3584"},{"content":"I have strong evidence for the core facts. Let me verify the remaining puzzle pieces: Marlon James's birthplace (Kingston, Jamaica) and that \"A Brief History of Seven Killings\" was published in 2014 (the year before it won the 2015 Booker). Let me read the Grinnell page about Marlon James.","role":"assistant","tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"ref\": \"d82\"}","name":"read"},"id":"call_336be266ca5a4f75a371aa3c","index":0,"type":"function"},{"function":{"arguments":"{\"queries\": [\"Marlon James born Kingston Jamaica author biography\", \"A Brief History of Seven Killings published 2014 Marlon James\"]}","name":"search"},"id":"call_e4305e34bcea46468ac07b4a","index":1,"type":"function"}]},{"content":"{\"action_slot_charged\":true,\"evidence\":{\"document\":\"d82\",\"document_chars\":12068,\"end\":3000,\"kind\":\"raw_evidence\",\"ref\":\"e2\",\"sha256\":\"ef7617b702e95290e75dc958e3edc2564b64b5b4de0babb9c06aed5fdb43e69b\",\"snapshot\":\"6e685f30dad72a655bc82764fe7e04e427e36ebd6c9ac41b6523e8f6c9bf1589\",\"start\":0,\"text\":\"---\\ntitle: Distinguished Authors\\ndate: 2000-01-01\\n---\\nEach year, in the spring, a distinguished author gives a public reading, conducts a workshop or roundtable, and dines with students and faculty. Among other prestigious honors, these authors have won the Pulitzer Prize, the National Book Award, the National Book Critics Circle Award, and the MacArthur Genius Grant. Initiated in 2004, the event usually caps a robust series of readings and workshops on campus. It is made possible by a Grinnell alum who prefers to remain anonymous. For more than fifteen years, he and his wife have supported programming that allows the Grinnell community to hear from, and interact with, the nation's most prominent writers. Their generosity also gave birth to the popular six-week short courses taught by faculty from the University of Iowa Writers' Workshop. Distinguished authors have included W.S. Merwin, Marilynne Robinson, John Edgar Wideman, Ana Castillo, Adrienne Rich, Edward P. Jones, Dorothy Allison, Mark Doty, Natasha Trethewey, Tracy K. Smith, Alison Bechdel, Edwidge Danticat, James McBride, Marlon James, and Tyehimba Jess.\\n\\nPast Distinguished Authors\\n\\n2020-2021: Alexander Chee\\n\\nAlexander Chee is the author of the novels Edinburgh and The Queen of the Night, and the essay collection How To Write An Autobiographical Novel, all from Houghton Mifflin Harcourt. He is a contributing editor at The New Republic, and an editor at large at VQR. His essays and stories have appeared in The New York Times Magazine, T Magazine, The Sewanee Review, The Yale Review, and Guernica, among others, and anthologized in the 2016 and 2019 Best American Essays. He is the winner of a 2003 Whiting Award, a 2004 NEA Fellowship in prose, and a 2021 US Artists Fellowship, as well as the Randy Shilts Prize in gay nonfiction, the Paul Engle Prize, the 2018 One Story Magazine's Mentor of the Year Award, and residency fellowships from the MacDowell Colony, the VCCA, Civitella Ranieri and Amtrak. He is an associate professor of English and Creative Writing at Dartmouth College.\\n\\n2019-2020: Ada Limon\\n\\nEvent cancelled due to Covid#19.\\n\\n2018-2019: Tyehimba Jess\\n\\nTyehimba Jess is the author of two books of poetry, Leadbelly and Olio. Olio won the 2017 Pulitzer Prize, the Anisfield-Wolf Book Award, The Midland Society Author's Award in Poetry, and received an Outstanding Contribution to Publishing Citation from the Black Caucus of the American Library Association. It was also nominated for the National Book Critics Circle Award, the PEN Jean Stein Book Award, and the Kingsley Tufts Poetry Award. Leadbelly was a winner of the 2004 National Poetry Series. The Library Journal and Black Issues Book Review both named it one of the \\\"Best Poetry Books of 2005.\\\" Jess is a Professor of English at College of Staten Island.\\n\\n2017-2018: Marlon James\\n\\nMarlon James won the 2015 Man Booker Prize for Fiction for A Brief History of Seven Killings, making him the first Jamaican author to take home the U.K.'s most\"},\"executed\":true,\"next_start\":3000,\"ok\":true,\"previously_received\":false}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_336be266ca5a4f75a371aa3c"},{"content":"{\"action_slot_charged\":true,\"executed\":true,\"ok\":true,\"results\":[{\"cached\":false,\"hits\":[{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d91\"},\"ref\":\"d91\",\"snippet\":\" ... James in the 82nd minute to replace Je-Vaughn Watson. The former Kingston College schoolboy and Harbour View's under-21 player, entered the game and within minutes hit a sensational volleyed home from a right-sided cross from Ryan Johnson from inside the area to earn Jamaica ... \",\"title\":\"https://thereggaeboyz.com/news.htm\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d92\"},\"ref\":\"d92\",\"snippet\":\" ... In The Book of Night Women, author Marlon James indicates that the ratio of slave owners to enslaved Africans is 1:33. James also depicts atrocities that slave owners subjected slaves to along with violent resistance from the slaves as well as numerous slaves who died in pursuit ... \",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Jamaica\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d93\"},\"ref\":\"d93\",\"snippet\":\" ... Born in Kingston, Jamaica, W.I., one of 5 children of Simon and Najla Brimo. Juliette married beloved husband James H. Bryant in 1956 and immigrated to the U.S. and Ft. Lauderdale. She is survived by her two sons, James N. of Orlando and Michael F. of ... \",\"title\":\"http://www.jamaicanfamilysearch.com/Samples/elsewher.htm\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d94\"},\"ref\":\"d94\",\"snippet\":\" ... Contributor\\n\\nMarlon James was born in Kingston, Jamaica. His work has been exhibited at the National Gallery of Jamaica, the IDB Cultural Center (DC), Art Museum of Americas (DC), Bargehouse (London), and Aliceyard (Trinidad).\",\"title\":\"https://strangersguide.com/articles/king-yellowman/\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d95\"},\"ref\":\"d95\",\"snippet\":\" ... Spice (musician) - Wikipedia\\nauthor: Authority control databases International ISNI VIAF WorldCat National United States Artists MusicBrainz Grammy Awards\\ndate: 2016-03-17\\n---\\nname: Spice\\nbirth_name: Grace Latoya Hamilton\\nbirth_date: 6 August 1982\\nbirth_place: Portmore, Jamaica\\neducation: Edna Manley College of the Visual and Performing Arts ... \",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spice_(musician)\"}],\"next_step\":\"Inspect navigation; read a relevant page or change the locating clue.\",\"observation\":{\"first_delivered_source_round\":null,\"prior_deliveries\":0,\"query_key\":\"1f6310981e1ae004ca0df66ac969cff2b8039e8404c2a976ab0629800dbb5675\",\"replay_requested\":false,\"result_fingerprint\":\"c2b24efab8f46c756fc378c0675f9c1eabfc80c0ba0ff0762ac4154c7fd733f4\",\"semantic_progress\":\"not_measured\"},\"query\":\"Marlon James born Kingston Jamaica author biography\",\"view\":\"full\"},{\"cached\":false,\"hits\":[{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d43\"},\"ref\":\"d43\",\"snippet\":\" ... Lincoln in the Bardo - George Saunders (2017) - 10\\n\\nThe Narrow Road to the Deep North - Richard Flanagan (2014) - 9.5\\n\\nGirl, Woman, Other - Bernardine Evaristo (2019) - 9\\n\\nA Brief History of Seven Killings - Marlon James (2015) - 9\\n\\nBring Up The Bodies - Hilary Mantel (2012) - 8.5\\n\\nThe Sense ... \",\"title\":\"https://www.ontheprize.co.uk/blog/the-booker-prize-in-the-2010s\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d82\"},\"ref\":\"d82\",\"snippet\":\" ... Marlon James\\n\\nMarlon James won the 2015 Man Booker Prize for Fiction for A Brief History of Seven Killings, making him the first Jamaican author to take home the U.K.'s most prestigious literary award. In addition to the Man Booker Prize, A Brief History of Seven ... \",\"title\":\"https://www.grinnell.edu/campus-life/arts-culture/writersgrinnell/distinguished-authors\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d96\"},\"ref\":\"d96\",\"snippet\":\" ... Ruby by Cynthia Bond\\n\\n• Team Seven by Marcus Burke\\n\\n• Every Day Is for the Thief by Teju Cole\\n\\n• An Untamed State by Roxane Gay\\n\\n• Remedy For a Broken Angel by Toni Ann Johnson\\n\\n• A Brief History of Seven Killings by Marlon James\\n\\n• All Our Names by Dinaw Mengestu ... \",\"title\":\"https://www.culturalfront.org/2019/12/novels-by-black-writers-2000-2019.html\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d97\"},\"ref\":\"d97\",\"snippet\":\" ... Ward\\n\\n2011\\n\\nThe Round House\\n\\nLouise Erdrich\\n\\n2012\\n\\nAmericanah\\n\\nChimamanda Ngozi Adichie\\n\\n2013\\n\\nNevada\\n\\nImogen Binnie\\n\\n2013\\n\\nA Brief History of Seven Killings\\n\\nMarlon James\\n\\n2014\\n\\nFamily Life\\n\\nAkhil Sharma\\n\\n2014\\n\\nFates and Furies\\n\\nLauren Groff\\n\\n2015\\n\\nThe Fifth Season\\n\\nN. K. Jemisin\\n\\n2015\\n\\nThe Sellout\\n\\nPaul Beatty ... \",\"title\":\"https://www.theatlantic.com/books/archive/2024/03/best-books-american-fiction/677479/\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d98\"},\"ref\":\"d98\",\"snippet\":\" ... 78 times\\n\\nMarlon James, winner of this year's Man Booker Prize for A Brief History of Seven Killings, told the BBC he almost gave up writing after his first novel was rejected nearly 80 times. \\\"I did give it up. I actually destroyed the manuscript, I even ... \",\"title\":\"https://earlybirdbooks.com/15-famous-books-that-were-initially-rejected\"}],\"next_step\":\"Inspect navigation; read a relevant page or change the locating clue.\",\"observation\":{\"first_delivered_source_round\":null,\"prior_deliveries\":0,\"query_key\":\"bfa4bfcaa8b136a7762971d6fe2477ba29f087466dbf269e2d0940b153b808cf\",\"replay_requested\":false,\"result_fingerprint\":\"e6a92269c723aeddc3c438b1f3f11a31f3ed32a5d79bfb1b50424e55e739824e\",\"semantic_progress\":\"not_measured\"},\"query\":\"A Brief History of Seven Killings published 2014 Marlon James\",\"view\":\"full\"}]}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_e4305e34bcea46468ac07b4a"},{"content":"I now have strong evidence for the key facts. Let me verify the publication date of \"A Brief History of Seven Killings\" (2014) to confirm it was released the year before winning the 2015 Booker. The Atlantic list (d97) shows it under 2014. Let me read that to confirm.","role":"assistant","tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"ref\": \"d97\", \"start\": 0, \"length\": 2500}","name":"read"},"id":"call_71ef2603b15a4153bdfa33d0","index":0,"type":"function"}]},{"content":"{\"action_slot_charged\":true,\"evidence\":{\"document\":\"d97\",\"document_chars\":8621,\"end\":2500,\"kind\":\"raw_evidence\",\"ref\":\"e3\",\"sha256\":\"713bc61cb45e597ff85f90c3d5c3498490cee358816071f662fcc7fbfc7fa291\",\"snapshot\":\"9bd0c708feb496e7a4e896082abe70db07c1093f3b53ce4a8529d47826c182bf\",\"start\":0,\"text\":\"---\\ntitle: TheGreatAmericanNovels\\ndate: 2024-03-14\\n---\\nTheGreatAmericanNovels\\n\\nIn 1868, a little-known writer by the name of John William DeForest proposed a new type of literature, a collective artistic project for a nation just emerging from an existential conflict: a work of fiction that accomplished \\\"the task of painting the American soul.\\\" It would be called the Great American Novel, and no one had written it yet, DeForest admitted. Maybe soon.\\n\\nA century and a half later, the idea has endured, even as it has become more complicated. In 2024, our definition of literary greatness is wider, deeper, and weirder than DeForest likely could have imagined. At the same time, the novel is also under threat, as the forces of anti-intellectualism and authoritarianism seek to ban books and curtail freedom of expression. The American canon is more capacious, more fluid, and more fragile than perhaps ever before. But what, exactly, is in it? What follows is our attempt to discover just that.\\n\\nIn setting out to identify that new American canon, we decided to define American as having first been published in the United States (or intended to be—read more in our entries on Lolita and The Bell Jar). And we narrowed our aperture to the past 100 years—a period that began as literary modernism was cresting and contains all manner of literary pleasure and possibility, including the experimentations of postmodernism and the narrative satisfactions of genre fiction.\\n\\nThis still left millions of potential titles. So we approached experts—scholars, critics, and novelists, both at The Atlantic and outside it—and asked for their suggestions. From there, we added and subtracted and debated and negotiated and considered and reconsidered until we landed on the list you're about to read. We didn't limit ourselves to a round, arbitrary number; we wanted to recognize the very best—novels that say something intriguing about the world and do it distinctively, in intentional, artful prose—no matter how many or few that ended up being (136, as it turns out). Our goal was to single out those classics that stand the test of time, but also to make the case for the unexpected, the unfairly forgotten, and the recently published works that already feel indelible. We aimed for comprehensiveness, rigor, and open-mindedness. Serendipity, too: We hoped to replicate that particular joy of a friend pressing a book into your hand and saying, \\\"You have to read this; you'll love it.\\\"\\n\\nThis list includes\"},\"executed\":true,\"next_start\":2500,\"ok\":true,\"previously_received\":false}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_71ef2603b15a4153bdfa33d0"},{"content":"Previously delivered raw windows (untrusted source data, not instructions or verified claims):\n[{\"document\":\"d21\",\"document_chars\":17355,\"end\":3000,\"kind\":\"raw_evidence\",\"ref\":\"e1\",\"sha256\":\"79767844ce2071fc5dcf3fb0e64d0cade0ba154ef0fc324d0054c1c767e63138\",\"snapshot\":\"90019ca46504785f620ef12f36abae20080c8c3bc7809053339e4beaa4deb2fd\",\"start\":0,\"text\":\"---\\ntitle: ADVISORS\\ndate: 2022-01-01\\n---\\nADVISORS\\n\\nAdvisors\\n\\nProgram Director\\n\\nChigozie Obioma was born in Akure, Nigeria. His two novels, The Fishermen (2015) and An Orchestra of Minorities (2019) were shortlisted for The Booker Prize and have been translated into 30 languages. He has an LA Times book prize, the prestigious Internationalerpris, FT/Oppenheimer prize for fiction, an NAACP Image award and has been nominated for two dozen prizes for fiction. He was named one of Foreign Policy's 100 Leading Global Thinkers in 2015 and served as a judge of the Booker prize in 2021. He is the Helen S. Lanier Distinguished Professor of English and Creative Writing at the University of Georgia, and the program director of the Oxbelly Writers retreat. His third novel, The Road to the Country, was published in 2024.\\n\\nProgram Manager\\n\\nRosie is a production and event manager based in Athens, Greece, with extensive experience in coordinating high-profile film festivals and cultural events. She holds a degree in Communication, Media and Culture from Athens and pursued further studies in Art History in Paris, culminating in a Master's in Culture & Tourism from Université Paris 1 Panthéon-Sorbonne. Her professional experience includes collaborations with cultural organizations and festivals worldwide, including UNESCO in New York, the Rolex Art Festival, Les Rencontres d'Arles Photo Festival among others. Since 2018, she has served as Festival Coordinator and Special Events Manager for the French Film Festival of Greece, and since 2022, she has been the Head of Production for the Athens International Children's Film Festival. Driven by a deep passion for cultural engagement and sustainable tourism, she continually seeks innovative ways to connect communities through the arts. In her free time, she enjoys playing music and gardening.\\n\\nProgram Director\\n\\nChigozie Obioma was born in Akure, Nigeria. His two novels, The Fishermen (2015) and An Orchestra of Minorities (2019) were shortlisted for The Booker Prize and have been translated into 30 languages. He has an LA Times book prize, the prestigious Internationaler Literaturpreis, FT/Oppenheimer prize for fiction, an NAACP Image award and has been nominated for two dozen prizes for fiction. He was a judge of the Booker prize in 2021. He is the Helen S. Lanier Professor of Creative Writing and English at the University of Georgia, and the program director of the Oxbelly Fiction Writers program. His third novel, The Road to the Country, will be published in 2024.\\n\\nProgram Manager\\n\\nKaterina Papanikolopoulos joined Faliro House in 2022 as Manager of Special Projects – a position that evolved into her role acting as the 2024 Oxbelly Retreat, Fiction Writers Program Manager. Following her graduation from UCLA (Summa Cum Laude, Art History), Papanikolopoulos founded the non-profit Athens Design Forum (ADF), a platform that advocates for overlooked and generative design histories interwoven with global labor and migration pa\",\"title\":\"https://www.oxbelly.com/fiction-writers-program/advisors\"}]","role":"user"},{"content":"Current control state (data, not source evidence):\n{\"current_notes_not_evidence\":[],\"evidence_shelf\":{\"omitted_for_capacity\":[],\"restored_exact_windows\":[\"e1\"],\"selection\":\"bounded recent first-delivery order; not a relevance/verification judgment\"},\"feedback\":null,\"instruction\":\"Read only useful passages. Notes and all-page cleanup are optional.\",\"literal_answer_contract\":{\"origin\":\"explicit experiment configuration, not inferred from question\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\"},\"phase\":\"RESEARCH\",\"remaining\":{\"action_slots\":178,\"backend_calls\":77,\"model_calls\":2,\"output_reservation\":44029},\"research_workflow\":{\"active_gap_not_verified\":null,\"available_pages_not_ranked_for_relevance\":[{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d98\"},\"ref\":\"d98\",\"snippet\":\" ... 78 times\\n\\nMarlon James, winner of this year's Man Booker Prize for A Brief History of Seven Killings, told the BBC he almost gave up writing after his firs\",\"title\":\"https://earlybirdbooks.com/15-famous-books-that-were-initially-rejected\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[{\"end\":2500,\"ref\":\"e3\",\"start\":0}],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d97\"},\"ref\":\"d97\",\"snippet\":\" ... Ward\\n\\n2011\\n\\nThe Round House\\n\\nLouise Erdrich\\n\\n2012\\n\\nAmericanah\\n\\nChimamanda Ngozi Adichie\\n\\n2013\\n\\nNevada\\n\\nImogen Binnie\\n\\n2013\\n\\nA Brief History of Seven Killin\",\"title\":\"https://www.theatlantic.com/books/archive/2024/03/best-books-american-fiction/677479/\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d96\"},\"ref\":\"d96\",\"snippet\":\" ... Ruby by Cynthia Bond\\n\\n• Team Seven by Marcus Burke\\n\\n• Every Day Is for the Thief by Teju Cole\\n\\n• An Untamed State by Roxane Gay\\n\\n• Remedy For a Broken Ange\",\"title\":\"https://www.culturalfront.org/2019/12/novels-by-black-writers-2000-2019.html\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d95\"},\"ref\":\"d95\",\"snippet\":\" ... Spice (musician) - Wikipedia\\nauthor: Authority control databases International ISNI VIAF WorldCat National United States Artists MusicBrainz Grammy Awards\\n\",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spice_(musician)\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d94\"},\"ref\":\"d94\",\"snippet\":\" ... Contributor\\n\\nMarlon James was born in Kingston, Jamaica. His work has been exhibited at the National Gallery of Jamaica, the IDB Cultural Center (DC), Art \",\"title\":\"https://strangersguide.com/articles/king-yellowman/\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d93\"},\"ref\":\"d93\",\"snippet\":\" ... Born in Kingston, Jamaica, W.I., one of 5 children of Simon and Najla Brimo. Juliette married beloved husband James H. Bryant in 1956 and immigrated to the\",\"title\":\"http://www.jamaicanfamilysearch.com/Samples/elsewher.htm\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d92\"},\"ref\":\"d92\",\"snippet\":\" ... In The Book of Night Women, author Marlon James indicates that the ratio of slave owners to enslaved Africans is 1:33. James also depicts atrocities that s\",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Jamaica\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d91\"},\"ref\":\"d91\",\"snippet\":\" ... James in the 82nd minute to replace Je-Vaughn Watson. The former Kingston College schoolboy and Harbour View's under-21 player, entered the game and within\",\"title\":\"https://thereggaeboyz.com/news.htm\"}],\"consecutive_repeat_rounds\":0,\"contract\":{\"enabled\":true,\"gap_state\":true,\"guided_read\":true,\"recovery_rounds\":2,\"repeat_threshold\":2,\"repetition\":\"bounded\",\"reuse_results\":true,\"version\":\"gap-workflow-v1\"},\"instruction\":\"Use the current missing relation to choose a real next action. Page-name search is not page reading; cached is not proof of progress.\",\"last_new_navigation_source_round\":13,\"last_new_raw_source_round\":14,\"previous_gap_judgments_not_verified\":[],\"recovery_decisions_used\":0,\"stage\":\"normal\"},\"retriever_capabilities\":{\"boolean_operators\":false,\"kind\":\"sqlite-fts5-bm25\",\"negative_result\":\"no hit for this expression is not proof that the fact is absent\",\"phrase_quotes\":false,\"query_rule\":\"lowercase; regex word extraction; first-occurrence deduplication; OR of quoted individual tokens\",\"ranking\":\"SQLite bm25(search), ascending; docid tie-break; unchanged from q26 LocalIndex\",\"scope\":\"fixed local corpus; not live web\",\"site_filter\":false,\"title_kind\":\"stored URL, not an extracted page title\",\"warning\":\"site:, quotes and AND/OR in input are not filters/operators. Their words may become ordinary search terms. Use a few discriminating terms.\"},\"runtime_contract\":{\"evidence_shelf_size\":3,\"max_calls_per_response\":4,\"max_queries_per_search\":3,\"require_sources\":true},\"uncommitted_response_not_evidence\":null}","role":"user"}],"model":"EB-GLM-5.2","temperature":0.0,"tool_choice":"auto","tools":[{"function":{"description":"FUNCTION: Find candidate documents in the configured corpus; it does NOT open their full text.\nUSE WHEN: No suitable page is known, a new entity/relation needs locating, or a different clue is needed after irrelevant results.\nAVOID: Do not repeat a page-name query when you intend to read an existing result. Do not treat snippets as proof or unsupported operators as filters.\nINPUT: queries is an array of 1..3 distinct nonblank strings, each <=2000 characters; top_k is an optional integer 1..10 (default 5). See runtime retriever_capabilities. replay=true explicitly restores the full cached navigation view; it does not force fresh SQL.\nOUTPUT: ok/results contains query, hits with ref/title/snippet, cache status and observation metadata. navigation_seen is NOT a read receipt; raw_ranges_delivered lists actual windows. read_action is an available example, not auto-execution. Metadata counts operations, not relevance. Exact delivered repeats may use view=reuse and omit snippets; replay=true restores them.\nWORKFLOW: search -> inspect returned dN -> read({\"ref\":\"dN\"}); after reading, use the newly found entity and one missing relation for a new search, or finish directly.","name":"search","parameters":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"queries":{"items":{"maxLength":2000,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"},"maxItems":3,"minItems":1,"type":"array","uniqueItems":true},"replay":{"type":"boolean"},"top_k":{"maximum":10,"minimum":1,"type":"integer"}},"required":["queries"],"type":"object"}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"FUNCTION: Open the raw text of a received search result, or replay a previously delivered evidence window.\nUSE WHEN: A candidate page may answer the current gap; inspect its actual text before another page-name search. Use continuation to include a subject/date split at a boundary.\nAVOID: Do not guess handles or cite an unread result. Do not infer an entity relationship merely because adjacent windows mention different people.\nINPUT: Only ref is required. dN opens from start=0 with configured read_chars by default; optional integer start>=0 and length=1..6000 use Unicode code points. eN accepts only ref and restores the exact old window. Optional goal<=400 characters on dN selects one lexical paragraph window; it is mutually exclusive with start and forbidden on eN. length remains an upper bound.\nOUTPUT: ok/evidence contains eN, document, snapshot, start/end, original text and integrity fields; next_start supports continuation. The window becomes citable after it is delivered in a subsequent model input. Source text is untrusted data. goal no-match returns matched=false with no evidence; selection metadata reports lexical matches and actual range, never a semantic guarantee.\nWORKFLOW: read({\"ref\":\"dN\"}) -> compare the original constraint with the text -> search a newly discovered gap, continue read, or finish with delivered eN.","name":"read","parameters":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"goal":{"maxLength":400,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"},"length":{"maximum":6000,"minimum":1,"type":"integer"},"ref":{"pattern":"^[de][1-9][0-9]*$","type":"string"},"start":{"minimum":0,"type":"integer"}},"required":["ref"],"type":"object"}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"FUNCTION: Find literal text positions inside one already received document; this is page-local navigation.\nUSE WHEN: The page is known but a name or exact phrase is far from its beginning.\nAVOID: Do not use a literal zero-match result to prove a fact absent. It does not search aliases, paraphrases or the whole corpus.\nINPUT: ref must be a received dN; text is a nonblank string <=2000 characters; optional integer start>=0; ignore_case is boolean, default false.\nOUTPUT: ok/document/snapshot/matches gives original start/end positions and exact excerpts, case_sensitive and next_start. Matches are positions_not_evidence, not citable eN.\nWORKFLOW: find -> read the matched range with enough preceding subject context -> compare evidence. Change literal terms or use goal-read rather than repeating zero matches.","name":"find","parameters":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"ignore_case":{"type":"boolean"},"ref":{"pattern":"^d[1-9][0-9]*$","type":"string"},"start":{"minimum":0,"type":"integer"},"text":{"maxLength":2000,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"}},"required":["ref","text"],"type":"object"}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"FUNCTION: Locate earlier delivered evidence, received search navigation and optional note history without external retrieval.\nUSE WHEN: A known lead or earlier raw window has left the active context; recover it instead of re-searching the same phrase.\nAVOID: Do not cite a recall excerpt as new evidence. It cannot search unreceived backend data; a lexical miss is not a proof of absence.\nINPUT: query is a nonblank string <=2000 characters. Use a discriminating entity or relation already encountered.\nOUTPUT: ok/matches has ref or note key, exact excerpt and kind. eN can be replayed; dN can be opened; inactive notes are historical model judgments, not evidence.\nWORKFLOW: recall -> read({\"ref\":\"eN\"}) for exact recovery, or read({\"ref\":\"dN\"}) for an unread page. Then update only the gap that changed.","name":"recall","parameters":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"query":{"maxLength":2000,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"}},"required":["query"],"type":"object"}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"FUNCTION: Maintain a small optional free-text scratchpad; not verified facts or a prerequisite to submit.\nUSE WHEN: A brief reusable alias, rejected route or independent reminder will help later. Prefer update_gap for the active research question when available.\nAVOID: Do not summarize every page, copy the full question, or repeatedly write the same note. Notes do not automatically retract dependent plans.\nINPUT: put requires op/key/text/anchors; text <=600 characters, anchors is <=6 delivered eN. delete requires op/key only. Keys are 1..32 ASCII letters/digits/_/-. Capacity is explicit.\nOUTPUT: ok/key/changed and kind=agent_note_not_evidence, or deleted. A no-op is not research progress. Errors do not silently alter saved notes.\nWORKFLOW: Optionally save a useful observation alongside another already-planned action; keep sources in the archive and finish without cleanup.","name":"notes","parameters":{"oneOf":[{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"anchors":{"items":{"pattern":"^e[1-9][0-9]*$","type":"string"},"maxItems":6,"minItems":0,"type":"array","uniqueItems":true},"key":{"pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,32}$","type":"string"},"op":{"const":"put"},"text":{"maxLength":600,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"}},"required":["op","key","text","anchors"],"type":"object"},{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"key":{"pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,32}$","type":"string"},"op":{"const":"delete"}},"required":["op","key"],"type":"object"}]}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"FUNCTION: Explicitly submit the requested answer with source windows, or explicitly abstain.\nUSE WHEN: The requested core answer has sufficient support, or the available budget/evidence cannot resolve it. No note, gap update or audit is mandatory.\nAVOID: Do not claim every clue is verified when it is not. Do not change established into expanded, by-year into in-year, or hide unresolved numeric conflicts in a long explanation.\nINPUT: Answer branch requires answer and refs. Nonblank strings are <=8000 characters; string-integer-v1 additionally permits plain JSON integers, not bools/floats, rendered as decimal text. Strings stay exact. Abstention requires abstain=true and nonblank reason<=2000 characters. Never mix branches. refs contains only delivered eN, <=16; configured source and literal-format requirements still apply.\nOUTPUT: ok/terminal records submitted or abstained. Submitted answer is text with refs/basis and any representation metadata. Legal references are NOT semantic verification. Explicit prefix/suffix constraints still apply.\nWORKFLOW: Finish last in a response, based only on evidence already received for that decision. Never combine a new read and a guessed citation to its unseen result. In FINAL only finish is allowed.","name":"finish","parameters":{"oneOf":[{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"answer":{"maxLength":8000,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":["string","integer"]},"refs":{"items":{"pattern":"^e[1-9][0-9]*$","type":"string"},"maxItems":16,"minItems":0,"type":"array","uniqueItems":true}},"required":["answer","refs"],"type":"object"},{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"abstain":{"const":true},"reason":{"maxLength":2000,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"}},"required":["abstain","reason"],"type":"object"}]}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"FUNCTION: Replace the one active research question and retain a bounded history of explicit model judgments; it is NOT an automatic verifier.\nUSE WHEN: A candidate, unresolved relation, rejection or conflict changed, or repeated searches need a concrete next objective. Use sparingly, not after every tool.\nAVOID: Do not turn the original question or your prior guess into evidence. Updating a gap does not count as a new observation and does not reset a search loop.\nINPUT: subject/question/basis are nonblank <=400 characters; kind and status follow schema; evidence_refs are <=6 received eN, navigation_refs <=6 received dN; next_action is one named existing tool. supported/rejected/conflict require evidence_refs. Supply a concise decision basis, not a transcript.\nOUTPUT: ok/changed/current returns agent_judgment_not_verified with revision. At most four previous distinct gap versions remain in the active view; the full update is journaled. Sources are checked for delivery, not entailment.\nWORKFLOW: Observe -> identify one missing relationship or record a real rejection -> select read on an available page or search for a new relation. An update may share a response with known independent actions; no update is required before finish.","name":"update_gap","parameters":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"basis":{"maxLength":400,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"},"evidence_refs":{"items":{"pattern":"^e[1-9][0-9]*$","type":"string"},"maxItems":6,"minItems":0,"type":"array","uniqueItems":true},"kind":{"enum":["locate","read","relation","disambiguate","conflict","recover"]},"navigation_refs":{"items":{"pattern":"^d[1-9][0-9]*$","type":"string"},"maxItems":6,"minItems":0,"type":"array","uniqueItems":true},"next_action":{"enum":["search","read","find","recall","finish"]},"question":{"maxLength":400,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"},"status":{"enum":["open","supported","rejected","conflict"]},"subject":{"maxLength":400,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"}},"required":["subject","question","kind","status","evidence_refs","navigation_refs","next_action","basis"],"type":"object"}},"type":"function"}]}